[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Jinja2 Templating in Flask"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Flask relies on the Jinja2 templating engine to dynamically generate HTML pages. Jinja2 allows you to embed Python-like expressions, control structures, and variables directly into your HTML files, making your web applications modular and maintainable."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up Your Flask Project"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use Jinja2 templates in Flask, your project directory must follow a specific structure. Flask automatically looks for HTML templates inside a folder named "templates" located in the root directory of your application."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a folder named templates in your project directory."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a static folder for CSS, JavaScript, and images if needed."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create your main Python application file, typically named app.py."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Rendering Templates from Routes"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Instead of returning raw strings from your Flask routes, use the render_template function to load HTML files and pass variables into them."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"from flask import Flask, render_template

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def home():
    username = "Alice"
    return render_template('index.html', name=username)

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Variables and Expressions in Jinja2"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inside your HTML templates, you can output variables passed from Flask by enclosing them in double curly braces. Jinja2 evaluates these expressions at runtime."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Home Page</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome, {{ name }}!</h1>
</body>
</html>"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Control Structures: Conditionals and Loops"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jinja2 supports standard programming logic such as if-statements and for-loops. These blocks are enclosed in curly-brace percent signs, like {% ... %}."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"{% if user_is_logged_in %}
    <p>Welcome back, user!</p>
{% else %}
    <p>Please log in to continue.</p>
{% endif %}

<ul>
{% for item in items %}
    <li>{{ item }}</li>
{% endfor %}
</ul>"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Template Inheritance"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Template inheritance is one of the most powerful features of Jinja2. It allows you to build a base skeleton layout containing all the common elements of your site (like headers, navigation bars, and footers) and define blocks that child templates can override."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"<!-- base.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>{% block title %}Default Title{% endblock %}</title>
</head>
<body>
    <nav>Site Navigation Bar</nav>
    <div class="content">
        {% block content %}{% endblock %}
    </div>
</body>
</html>"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Child templates can then inherit from the base layout using the extends tag and populate the designated blocks."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"{% extends "base.html" %}

{% block title %}Dashboard{% endblock %}

{% block content }}
    <h2>User Dashboard</h2>
    <p>This content is injected into the base layout.</p>
{% endblock %}"}]
